## 3. Supabase S3-compat specifics

### 3.1 Presign expiry vs the 24 h window
- SigV4 query-string presigns can run up to 7 days, so a 24 h expiry that matches the multipart window is fine and removes an entire class of "URL expired mid-part" failures. Set `timeoutIntervalForResource` on the session **≤ the presign expiry** so tasks don't outlive their URLs.
- Blast radius of a leaked presigned part URL is one write to one part of one uploadId. Acceptable.
- **Sign only `host`.** If native adds any header that the signature included in `SignedHeaders` (`Content-Type`, `Content-MD5`, `x-amz-*`), the PUT gets a 403. Keep the request bare: method, URL, body, `Content-Length`. Never add `Authorization`.

### 3.2 ETags
- Treat ETags as **opaque strings**. Pass them back to Complete exactly as received (quotes included). A completed multipart's ETag is `<md5-of-md5s>-<N>`, not an MD5 of the content — don't use it for integrity.
- Since `ListParts` is authoritative at Complete time, client-side ETag capture is just a hint. That's the right design. Verification of the final object should be `HeadObject` → `Content-Length == source size` (you already do this in 0a).

### 3.3 ListParts reconciliation edge cases
- **Same part number uploaded twice** overwrites (last write wins). Retries are safe.
- **Part sizes are not required to be uniform** — only ≥ 5 MiB for all but the last. This lets you shrink part size mid-upload if failure rates are high (§3.4). The client owns the byte-range → part-number map; persist it in native SQLite and reconcile `ListParts` *sizes* against it, not just part numbers.
- **Paginate `ListParts`** (`IsTruncated` / `PartNumberMarker`) even though 235 parts fits in one page. Cheap insurance.
- **`NoSuchUpload` on `ListParts` or `UploadPart`** = the window lapsed or the upload was aborted → surface your explicit "expired" state.
- **Captive portals.** Gym wifi often returns `200` HTML (or a `302`) for any request before the user taps "Accept." To `URLSession` that's a successful task. Your delegate must check `HTTPURLResponse.statusCode` *and* the presence of an `ETag` header; a 2xx with no ETag is a failure to retry. Same for `403` (expired/bad signature → fresh presign), `5xx` (retry with backoff), `400 InvalidPart` (regenerate that part).
- **Never trust a client-supplied `uploadId`/`objectKey` at Complete.** Record the plan server-side (a `video_uploads` row: coach, team, object key, uploadId, status, created_at) when you create it; Complete takes only your row id and authorizes against it. This is also what lets a cron job abort orphaned uploads and reconcile disk.

### 3.4 Part size on flaky wifi
Per-part overhead on S3 is negligible above ~16 MiB, so the cost of larger parts is entirely on the failure side: a part that dies at 90% restarts from zero, and a wifi↔cellular switch kills every in-flight part. With 15 GB files the total part count is not a constraint (10,000 max; 64 MiB → ~235 parts).

Recommendation: **64 MiB default, 3 concurrent** (`HTTPMaximumConnectionsPerHost = 3`; the system may use fewer), and make the size adaptive: if more than ~20% of recent parts fail, drop remaining parts to 32 MiB. Keep 128 MiB in the A/B, but expect it to lose on deliberately bad wifi. Expect 256 MiB to lose clearly.

