## 4. Background URLSession pitfalls

### 4.1 Things you have right
- `background(withIdentifier:)`, `isDiscretionary = false`, `sessionSendsLaunchEvents = true`.
- Recreating the session on native launch *before* RN starts, forwarding `handleEventsForBackgroundURLSession`.
- Native SQLite as system of record; reconcile local ∩ `getAllTasks()` ∩ `ListParts` on relaunch.
- Enqueue while foregrounded.

### 4.2 Things to add or watch
- **Tasks created while the app is in the background may be treated as discretionary** regardless of `isDiscretionary`. Discretionary tasks wait for favorable conditions (wifi, battery). This is the hidden cost of the rolling-window refill: refills triggered from a background wake can be deferred. So: enqueue the full plan up front whenever disk permits (§2.1); when it doesn't, refill preferentially on foreground returns and treat background refills as best-effort. Tell the user when the upload is waiting on the app being opened.
- **The rate-limit trap is about wakes, not task count.** Enqueuing 100–250 tasks is fine (Photos does far more). The system launches the app roughly once when the enqueued tasks have all completed or need attention, so a rolling window of 12 parts on a 235-part plan is ~20 wakes over several hours — acceptable. What triggers throttling: taking too long in the wake, or **failing to call the stored `completionHandler`** in `urlSessionDidFinishEvents(forBackgroundURLSession:)` on the main thread. Do the minimum in the wake (record results, delete part files, enqueue next batch, call the handler).
- **One session per identifier, ever.** Creating a second `URLSession` with the same identifier is undefined. Make the native session a singleton; this bites hard with Expo dev reloads/Fast Refresh.
- **Handle the cancel reasons.** On force-quit, tasks come back on next launch with `NSURLErrorCancelled` and `NSURLErrorBackgroundTaskCancelledReasonKey` = `userForceQuit` (also `backgroundUpdatesDisabled`, `insufficientSystemResources`). Force-quit also means iOS will **not** relaunch you for session events — the user must open the app. Show "Upload paused because the app was closed — open to resume" rather than a generic failure.
- **Progress while suspended is invisible.** On foreground, rebuild progress from `getAllTasks()` → `countOfBytesSent` per task plus completed parts in SQLite. Progress on a task resets when the system retries it; smooth this in UI.
- **Expo wiring.** `handleEventsForBackgroundURLSession` needs an `ExpoAppDelegateSubscriber` (or config plugin) — it won't work in Expo Go, and background session behavior in the Simulator is not representative. Real device only, which you already know.
- **Extend the device test matrix** beyond foreground→background→lock→wifi↔cellular→force-quit→relaunch with: Low Power Mode on; Low Data Mode on; Background App Refresh off; captive-portal wifi; phone reboot mid-upload (tasks survive reboot? verify); disk-almost-full; and the "start at gym, resume at home 6 h later" run. **Run the full matrix with a real 15 GB file.** 2–5 GB tests will not surface the disk and time failures.

