## 6. Android sanity check (later)

No objection to the shape, with three notes:

- **Android 14+ User-Initiated Data Transfer jobs** are the right primitive (`setUserInitiated(true)`, `RUN_USER_INITIATED_JOBS`, must be started while the app is visible or from a notification, must show a notification, should call `setEstimatedNetworkBytes`). They're exempt from the limit below.
- **Android 14 caps `dataSync` foreground services at ~6 hours per 24 h.** A 15 GB upload on 5 Mbps exceeds that. So the "WorkManager + foreground service" fallback is only safe on Android 12–13; on 14+ it must be UIDT.
- **Android doesn't need part files.** OkHttp can send a byte range of a file as a `RequestBody`, so the disk problem in §2.1 disappears there. Same Edge Function protocol, same reconcile logic. Expect OEM battery managers (Samsung, Xiaomi) to kill long jobs; a UIDT notification helps but plan for reconcile-on-open as the norm.

